WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:03.020
Film Courage: Mark, I believe you’ve said
previously that every director must also be

00:00:03.020 --> 00:00:04.290
a writer.

00:00:04.290 --> 00:00:10.090
So does that mean that every director should
rewrite (in some sense) the screenplay of

00:00:10.090 --> 00:00:11.200
the movie that they are directing?

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:13.420
Mark W. Travis: Oh…what a great question.

00:00:13.420 --> 00:00:15.050
But there are two questions.

00:00:15.050 --> 00:00:17.990
You always hit me with two questions [laughs].

00:00:17.990 --> 00:00:21.430
First of all, every director…I’m going
to do every director should be a writer.

00:00:21.430 --> 00:00:22.430
Yes!

00:00:22.430 --> 00:00:24.300
Every director should write.

00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:28.369
Whether they write well or not is not important.

00:00:28.369 --> 00:00:31.099
I think every director should understand the
writing process.

00:00:31.099 --> 00:00:33.840
That’s why we do Write Your Life.

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:39.160
But also the screenwriting process which is
different and to understand and appreciate

00:00:39.160 --> 00:00:44.670
the challenges I’m trying to get a story
into what I call the 2-Hour Haiku.

00:00:44.670 --> 00:00:51.790
You know, it’s like reducing it down to…well,
we can talk about that later.

00:00:51.790 --> 00:00:55.800
But in terms of the director rewriting.

00:00:55.800 --> 00:00:57.469
No!

00:00:57.469 --> 00:00:58.930
Rewriting a script?

00:00:58.930 --> 00:01:01.660
It’s not the director’s job to rewrite.

00:01:01.660 --> 00:01:04.540
It is the director’s job to work with a
writer.

00:01:04.540 --> 00:01:11.409
I’m a very passionate protector of the writer
and I think if the writer has brought a script

00:01:11.409 --> 00:01:15.640
to a point where a director is interested
in doing it, then they should keep working

00:01:15.640 --> 00:01:16.640
with the writer.

00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:20.609
I don’t understand this getting rid of writers
or bringing in other people to rewrite.

00:01:20.609 --> 00:01:26.659
I do understand the collaboration of a director
and writer working and then eventually with

00:01:26.659 --> 00:01:30.639
the actors and everybody else staying involved.

00:01:30.639 --> 00:01:34.160
When I’ve done films the writer stays all
the way through the making of the film, all

00:01:34.160 --> 00:01:36.499
the way through production and post-production.

00:01:36.499 --> 00:01:39.520
They are part of the storytelling team.

00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:40.990
I have my job.

00:01:40.990 --> 00:01:41.990
They have their job.

00:01:41.990 --> 00:01:43.659
I like having the writer on the set.

00:01:43.659 --> 00:01:48.909
I’m shooting something…there was one film
I was doing which we we’re having trouble

00:01:48.909 --> 00:01:49.909
with the a scene.

00:01:49.909 --> 00:01:54.920
I remember this…the two writers were there…it
was a writing team.

00:01:54.920 --> 00:01:58.130
And we couldn't get it to work and I told
them…I said “I am going to improvise the

00:01:58.130 --> 00:01:59.130
scene.

00:01:59.130 --> 00:02:00.130
Hold on.”

00:02:00.130 --> 00:02:01.400
And they went “Okay.”

00:02:01.400 --> 00:02:06.600
And I went back to the actors and I took them
through a quick process and then we started

00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:08.959
to improvise the scene and I remember the
writer going “Yes!

00:02:08.959 --> 00:02:10.720
You’ve got it!”

00:02:10.720 --> 00:02:11.970
No.

00:02:11.970 --> 00:02:14.290
The fact that the writers said “Yes!

00:02:14.290 --> 00:02:16.260
That’s where we’re going,” I felt great.

00:02:16.260 --> 00:02:17.650
It wasn’t me rewriting the scene.

00:02:17.650 --> 00:02:20.689
It wasn’t even the actors rewriting the
scene.

00:02:20.689 --> 00:02:22.750
The writers were there.

00:02:22.750 --> 00:02:28.530
I’m trying to serve their initial vision
of the film, combined with mine, combined

00:02:28.530 --> 00:02:31.650
with the actors trying to reach the characters.

00:02:31.650 --> 00:02:39.550
So I don’t like the idea (although it happens
a lot in Europe, which you may know) and I’ve

00:02:39.550 --> 00:02:43.000
run it by a lot of friends because I teach
a lot in Europe (especially in Germany).

00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:47.170
And a lot of friends who are very, very successful
writers there and it’s a constant problem.

00:02:47.170 --> 00:02:51.939
They will write a script (say for television)
and a director comes on and the director does

00:02:51.939 --> 00:02:52.939
a rewrite.

00:02:52.939 --> 00:02:56.910
The first thing they do is rewrite it.

00:02:56.910 --> 00:03:06.230
I directed a little bit in Germany and at
one point was going to direct something and

00:03:06.230 --> 00:03:11.459
the production company sent me the script
and said “When are we going to get your

00:03:11.459 --> 00:03:14.570
director’s script?” and I said “What
do you mean?”

00:03:14.570 --> 00:03:17.150
I’m reading the script and am saying “Yes,
I want to do this.

00:03:17.150 --> 00:03:19.310
What do you mean the director’s script?

00:03:19.310 --> 00:03:23.409
What they meant was: we expect you to rewrite
it.

00:03:23.409 --> 00:03:29.329
So this was the producers expecting the director
to rewrite the script that they had bought

00:03:29.329 --> 00:03:32.360
and they wanted to produce.

00:03:32.360 --> 00:03:33.360
And I said “No.

00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:34.360
I am not going to rewrite it.

00:03:34.360 --> 00:03:36.659
I’m going to work with a writer.”

00:03:36.659 --> 00:03:43.970
So I don’t encourage directors to think
they have to rewrite it.

00:03:43.970 --> 00:03:49.180
I’ve seen too many times (especially in
Europe) directors rewriting and reworking

00:03:49.180 --> 00:03:54.209
a script and it was really clear what they
were doing, unconsciously (I think) or maybe

00:03:54.209 --> 00:03:55.239
consciously.

00:03:55.239 --> 00:03:59.790
They were creating a script that they knew
how to direct.

00:03:59.790 --> 00:04:05.120
And by doing that they reduced it or shifted
it from the initial intention rather than

00:04:05.120 --> 00:04:13.590
trying to serve the script that was there
initially, they altered it to something that

00:04:13.590 --> 00:04:14.590
they could handle.

00:04:14.590 --> 00:04:17.430
Film Courage: And then the story lost its
essence because…

00:04:17.430 --> 00:04:24.509
Mark W. Travis: Lost this one very important
project which a friend of mine had written

00:04:24.509 --> 00:04:30.320
and she had a powerful script and it was changed
so dramatically into the style of film that

00:04:30.320 --> 00:04:32.190
the director felt comfortable in doing.

00:04:32.190 --> 00:04:36.199
He had been hired to do a film that he wasn’t
really comfortable with, so he changed into

00:04:36.199 --> 00:04:37.990
something…and it changed totally.

00:04:37.990 --> 00:04:41.890
It lost (to me) the essence of what was there
originally.

00:04:41.890 --> 00:04:44.620
It turned out fine in the end but it was a
whole different story.

00:04:44.620 --> 00:04:46.431
It wasn’t what was there originally.

00:04:46.431 --> 00:04:47.029
Question for the Viewers: What are your thoughts
on a director rewriting a script?

